=== Settings ===

Your settings live in one file outside the BotWikiAwk directory:

  ~/.config/botwikiawk/settings     (or the file named by the environment variable BOTWIKIAWK_SETTINGS)

The files in ~/BotWikiAwk/lib are never edited, so BotWikiAwk can be updated without losing
anything. The file has one "key value" per line, # for comments. For example:

  Exe.wget           /usr/bin/wget
  Agent              MyBot/1.0 (User:MyName; me@example.com)
  StopButton         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:MyName/button
  UserPage           https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User:MyName
  bot.default.home   /home/me/BotWikiAwk/bots/%s/

The keys are described at the top of lib/syscfg.awk (paths to programs, Agent, email) and
lib/botwiki.awk (stop button, user page, bot locations).

setup.sh creates the file and adds the path of each dependency it finds, as Exe.<name>. If it
can't find a unix tool it says so. This is not important, unless you need that tool for your bot.
For example 'shuf' is rarely needed so don't worry that it's missing - unless you need it, then
install it and add "Exe.shuf /path/to/shuf" to the settings file. Running setup.sh again only adds
keys that are missing, it does not change your settings.

=== Your own functions ===

Put functions you want in every bot in ~/BotWikiAwk/lib/local.awk. syscfg.awk loads it into every
program that uses BotWikiAwk. It ships empty and BotWikiAwk never changes it, so git pull leaves
your copy alone. Don't add functions to the other files in lib/ - an update would replace them.

=== Email ===

BWA has support for sending email from any computer that has curl installed no other
dependencies are reqired. You will only need an upstream SMTP server such as what an ISP
provides for POP3/IMAP email clients.

Email settings are the keys Exe.email_auth, Exe.from_email and Exe.to_email in the settings
file - see lib/syscfg.awk for instructions.

You can test email is working with this command:

  awk -ilibrary 'BEGIN{email(Exe["from_email"], Exe["to_email"], "Hi from my computer", "")}'

=== Bot cfg ===

When creating a new bot, it will require configuration information. There are two 
locations cfg information can be placed, your choice. Each bot chooses one of the
two locations. Diffrent bots can use different location methods.

The advantage of location 2 is it keeps all information about the bot in a single 
file which makes its more portable and easier to maintain. Location 1 is the 
original design of BWA and maintained for backward compatibility. 

 ==== Location 1 ====

In the settings file, each bot can have its home directory and Agent. For example:

      bot.accdate.home    /home/admin/bots/accdate/
      bot.accdate.agent   AccdateBot/1.0 (User:MyName)

A bot without its own lines uses bot.default.home and bot.default.agent, where %s is the BotName
and %u is UserPage. Without those, the Agent is UserPage " (ask me about " BotName ")".

Then at the top of the program, define the bot's name like this:

        BEGIN {
          BotName = "accdate"
        }

This block comes before the @include statements.

 ==== Location 2 ====

All cfg info resides in a BEGIN{} section at the top of the bot program. Nothing goes
in the settings file

The configuration looks like this. Notice it comes before the @include statements

	BEGIN { # Bot cfg
	
	  _defaults = "home      = /home/admin/bots/accdate/ \
	               email     = sample@example.com \
	               version   = 1.0 \
	               copyright = 2024"

	  asplit(G, _defaults, "[ ]*[=][ ]*", "[ ]{9,}")
	  BotName = "accdate"
	  Home = G["home"]
	  Agent = "Ask me about " BotName " - " G["email"]    
	  Engine = 0

	}

	@include "botwiki.awk"
	@include "library.awk"

In this BEGIN{} block you can define the bot's home directory, email, BotName and Engine.

The options: 

        . _defaults - spacing is significant, there can be no blank spaces after each \, no tabs, the
           home directory should end with a / as seen above. See the ~/BotWikiAwl/example-bots for
           real examples. makebot can also create this section for you automatically. 
	. 'BotName' is the name of the directory defined in the _defaults which is also the name
           of the awk program. Thus, the BotName of "accdate" will be in the directory /home/admin/bots/accdate
           which contains a program accdate.awk
	. 'Agent' is the user-agent used by wget/curl - or set a global Agent in the settings file
	. 'StopButton' is the URL to your bot's stop button page. If included here, it overrides the
	   global StopButton in the settings file - set StopButton = "none" for a bot that should not check it.
	. 'Engine' is the parallel/concurrency method used for this bot:

	     0 = none or GNU Parallel on a single computer
	     1 = (Deprecated) Toolforge Array (single job submitted for all articles - fastest method)
	     2 = (Deprecated) Toolforge Jsub  (one job submitted per article - slower)       
	     3 = Non-framework eg. using upload() instead of runbot.awk       

           It is recommended to use Engine 0 for bots using the BotWikiAwk framework (eg. runbot, etc)
           For bots that are standalone tools, use Engine 3.

	   NOTE: As of 2024, Toolforge no longer supports SGE (Sun Grid Engine). BotWikiAwk will only
	         work in modes 0 or 3, for now. A future version of BWE will support the new
	         method of concurrency at Toolforge.
